---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Playground from '../components/figures/Playground';
---
<Base title="Playground" description="Run and explain SQL against the book's example schema in your browser." current="playground">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width: 1100px">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/">Course</a><span>›</span><span>Playground</span></p>
    <h1 style="font-size: clamp(28px, 4vw, 36px); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -.02em; margin: 0 0 var(--s2)">Run it yourself</h1>
    <p class="lede">A real SQLite database with the book's <a href="/sql/example-schema/">example schema</a>, running inside your browser. Nothing is sent anywhere. Create indexes, run queries, read the plan.</p>
    <Playground client:load title="scratch" sql={`SELECT first_name, last_name\n  FROM employees\n WHERE subsidiary_id = 20\n   AND last_name = 'Winand';`} />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Reading SQLite plans</h2>
      <p>SQLite's <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> is short. The lines that matter:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>SCAN employees</code> is a full table scan: every row is read.</li>
        <li><code>SEARCH employees USING INDEX idx (col=?)</code> is an index access. The columns in parentheses are the access predicates, the part of the where clause that narrows the scanned index range.</li>
        <li><code>USING COVERING INDEX</code> means the index alone answered the query, no table access.</li>
        <li><code>USE TEMP B-TREE FOR ORDER BY</code> means an explicit sort happened. An index in the right order makes it disappear.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Other engines are more verbose. The <a href="/sql/explain-plan/">execution plan reference</a> covers each one.</p>
      <h2>Things to try</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Run the query above, then <code>CREATE INDEX emp_sub_name ON employees (subsidiary_id, last_name);</code> and run it again. Compare the plan and the time.</li>
        <li>Try <code>WHERE UPPER(last_name) = 'WINAND'</code>. Then create an index on <code>UPPER(last_name)</code>.</li>
        <li>Page through sales with <code>ORDER BY sale_date DESC LIMIT 10 OFFSET 5000</code>, then with a seek condition instead of the offset.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
